{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"63f77566-eb15-408d-8ce0-38b4e9e47706","status":"SUCCESS","response":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me analyze the bugs and the required fix:\n\n**Current bugs:**\n1. **`publish()`** — generates `date` in UTC, ignoring the author's timezone (`client_tz`). A Buenos Aires author posting at 23:30 (UTC-3) gets the next UTC day.\n2. **`display_date()`** — converts the stored date to the *viewer's* timezone, which means two people in different zones see different dates on the same post. The date should be opaque/display as-is.\n3. **`feed()`** — sorts by `date` (author's calendar date), but should sort by `received_at` (UTC submission timestamp) to maintain true submission order.\n\n**The fix per the guide:**\n1. In `publish()`, derive `date` from the author's local clock (using `client_tz`), and store a UTC `received_at` for ordering. Also handle missing `client_tz` gracefully (fall back to deriving local date from author's tz, not plain UTC).\n2. `display_date()` returns the stored `date` directly — no conversion.\n3. `feed()` sorts by `received_at` descending, with `date` as fallback.\n\nNow let me update the tests to cover the new scenarios (Tokyo UTC+9, Buenos Aires UTC-3, legacy no-date caller, feed ordering by `received_at`), then fix `journal.py`:\nNow update the tests to cover all the scenarios described in the guide:\nAll 11 tests pass. Here's a summary of what was changed and why:\n\n---\n\n## What was wrong\n\n| Location | Bug |\n|---|---|\n| [`publish()`](file:///tmp/h-hy4IDT/journal.py#L16-L29) | `date` was derived from **UTC**, ignoring `client_tz`. An author in UTC-3 posting at 23:30 got the **next** UTC date. |\n| [`display_date()`](file:///tmp/h-hy4IDT/journal.py#L33-L39) | Date was **re-converted** to the viewer's timezone, so different viewers saw different dates on the same post. |\n| [`feed()`](file:///tmp/h-hy4IDT/journal.py#L42-L44) | Sorted by `date` (author's calendar date), which broke ordering when two authors shared a calendar date but submitted at different UTC moments. |\n\n---\n\n## The fix (per the guide's pattern)\n\n**`date` is content** — captured once from the author's local clock, stored verbatim, never converted:\n\n```python\n# publish(): derive from author's tz, not UTC\nauthor_local = utc_now.astimezone(ZoneInfo(client_tz))\npost[\"date\"] = author_local.date().isoformat()\n```\n\n**`received_at` is metadata** — a UTC instant used only for ordering:\n```python\npost[\"received_at\"] = utc_now.isoformat()\n```\n\n**`display_date()`** returns the opaque string, `viewer_tz` accepted but ignored:\n```python\nreturn post[\"date\"]   # no conversion — ever\n```\n\n**`feed()`** sorts by `received_at` (true submission order), with `date` as tie-breaker:\n```python\nkey=lambda p: (p[\"received_at\"], p[\"date\"])\n```\n","duration_seconds":65.574995,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":14848,"output_tokens":4004,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":95294,"total_tokens":18852}}}
